w this project together so want a  thing to remember right I love this  quote essentially all models are wrong  some are useful right very important to  remember attributed to statistician  George box and this is something that I  think is absolutely true that no matter  how good a machine learning model is it  is going to get things wrong no matter  what  what is our tolerance for the machine  learning model getting things wrong we  we cannot help it it will happen I can  guarantee it  even with you know 99.99999% confidence  you're gonna hit that point zero zero  zero one percent of the time right and  then like I said inputs change stimulus  changes so what happens in these new  novel instances where suddenly we don't  know what's gonna what how the model is  going to react to these new instances  that we haven't seen in the past right  so one of the things that I really  really really recommend to people is to  take risk mitigation plans very  seriously  okay machine learning models rarely get  to function by themselves without any  kind of guardrails in place on either  side right so we want to make sure that  we have a risk mitigation plan make sure  that we're getting good inputs make sure  that we're getting reasonable outputs  making sure that our machine learning  model is also augmented with business  logic so that we're not doing anything  that's obviously wrong right and it  seems pretty obvious but at the same  time I think that sometimes data science  is billed as being this magic bullet  that I can do anything and that is magic  right and that we are always able to  create perfect models that are going to  and you know make everything all better  and we're all going to become rich and  retire and do open source full-time  right but that's probably not the case  in the real world we're going to have to  be very very careful about how we're  using it and also evaluate whether or  not the application that we're writing  this machine learning model for i